WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.368
Iterators are one of the main ways C++ connects algorithms to data.

2
00:00:04.368 --> 00:00:11.284
They let code walk through vectors, strings, maps, sets, lists, custom containers, and range views with a common vocabulary.

3
00:00:11.284 --> 00:00:16.016
That vocabulary is powerful because it separates traversal from the exact container implementation.

4
00:00:16.016 --> 00:00:19.292
It also comes with rules that code must respect.

5
00:00:19.292 --> 00:00:23.660
An iterator is not a permanent permission slip to access an element.

6
00:00:23.660 --> 00:00:28.392
It is meaningful only while the range, element, and traversal state remain valid.

7
00:00:28.392 --> 00:00:30.576
A container mutation can invalidate it.

8
00:00:30.576 --> 00:00:32.396
A temporary range can disappear.

9
00:00:32.396 --> 00:00:36.036
A stored iterator can outlive the object it refers to.

10
00:00:36.036 --> 00:00:42.588
A loop can accidentally step to the end and then dereference a marker that is not an element.

11
00:00:42.588 --> 00:00:48.776
Iterator defects can produce crashes, incorrect results, skipped elements, repeated processing, corrupted application state, and undefined behavior.

12
00:00:48.776 --> 00:00:52.416
In security-conscious engineering, those are not minor style problems.

13
00:00:52.416 --> 00:00:59.332
They are reliability and correctness risks that can weaken defensive controls, confuse auditing, and make incidents harder to diagnose.

14
00:00:59.332 --> 00:01:02.244
Safe iterator use starts with a preventive mindset.

15
00:01:02.244 --> 00:01:03.700
Keep range boundaries clear.

16
00:01:03.700 --> 00:01:05.520
Know when mutation changes validity.

17
00:01:05.520 --> 00:01:09.160
Treat stored or reused iterators as design decisions, not conveniences.

18
00:01:09.160 --> 00:01:12.072
Prefer traversal patterns that are easy to review.

19
00:01:12.072 --> 00:01:18.624
Iterator safety is part of normal defensive C++ work because it protects the assumptions that algorithms rely on.

20
00:01:18.624 --> 00:01:24.084
The goal of this course is not to memorize every corner of the standard library.

21
00:01:24.084 --> 00:01:33.184
The practical goal is to recognize the situations where iterator validity is at risk and to build habits that make those risks visible in code,

22
00:01:33.184 --> 00:01:34.640
tests, diagnostics, and review.
